00:00:28.223 --> 00:00:29.640
AMIT GANDHI: So
today, we're going

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:35.020
to be doing a little hands-on
wind turbine building activity.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:36.660
So by the end--

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:39.250
if you guys remember
from the first class,

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:41.550
lab session, where
we cut and sawed

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:44.280
things apart using
different hand tools,

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.350
well, the end
product is supposed

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:50.050
to be something that
looks like this.

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:50.850
It's pretty simple.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:51.795
The way it works is--

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:02.270
the way it works is you
have this wooden dowel,

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:04.190
and you're mounting
a wind turbine on it.

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:07.310
When you blow on this in
this direction, it spins.

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:11.000
It has magnets
attached to the bottom.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.780
And then you're making coils
with electromagnetic wire.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:17.360
And so as the magnets
spin by the coils,

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:23.523
you're inducing a current
and generating electricity.

00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:27.252
STUDENT: Hey, how are you doing?

00:01:27.252 --> 00:01:28.430
STUDENT: Not bad.

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:29.630
What are we doing here?

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:32.320
STUDENT: We are just starting
to assemble this together,

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:34.630
so it's pretty vague.

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:36.760
STUDENT: Oh, this is--

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.800
so do we just have to cut the
bottles in half right now?

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:41.487
STUDENT: I think so.

00:01:41.487 --> 00:01:42.570
STUDENT: Oh, I don't know.

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:44.170
Does this not stay on here?

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:46.520
STUDENT: No, it says blue here.

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:47.910
But this is--

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:49.820
I don't know how--

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:52.510
I don't think I can
screw this in like that.

00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:53.810
Stupid thing.

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:55.240
STUDENT: It does
smell like soap.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:56.532
It actually smells really good.

00:01:56.532 --> 00:01:58.070
Why scent is that?

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:00.620
STUDENT: When it's turning,
it's going to smell.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:04.400
STUDENT: It's going to
be like an air freshener.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:07.215
STUDENT: It didn't have
this box originally.

00:02:07.215 --> 00:02:09.590
AMIT GANDHI: So if you guys
feel like changing the design

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:11.300
at all, because you think
something makes more sense,

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:13.460
or you want to make any
improvements to the design,

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:14.465
you should feel free to.

00:02:14.465 --> 00:02:16.340
Don't feel like have to
follow the directions

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:17.700
like every step of the way.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:18.200
STUDENT: OK.

00:02:22.452 --> 00:02:27.908
[SIDE CONVERSATIONS]

00:03:01.372 --> 00:03:02.270
STUDENT: I got it.

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:03.452
STUDENT: Nice.

00:03:03.452 --> 00:03:05.907
STUDENT: Wow, that is excellent.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:25.660
STUDENT: We have to-- do we
have to do a piece between them?

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:26.830
STUDENT: We need two of these.

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:29.372
STUDENT: Yeah, but do we need
something in between these two?

00:03:29.372 --> 00:03:30.530
STUDENT: I don't think so.

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:32.781
STUDENT: I think the idea was--

00:03:32.781 --> 00:03:36.018
STUDENT: 61, 62, 63, 64.

00:03:36.018 --> 00:03:38.358
STUDENT: The coils are
going to end up being--

00:03:38.358 --> 00:03:39.804
[SIDE CONVERSATIONS]

00:03:39.804 --> 00:03:44.457
STUDENT: 68, 69, 70,
71, 72, 73, 74, 75.

00:03:56.030 --> 00:03:59.810
AMIT GANDHI: Wait, are you
doing all four coils first?

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:03.530
You should do all
four coils at once.

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:06.560
But make sure you leave space,
like wire between the coils

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:08.196
so you do separate.

00:04:08.196 --> 00:04:10.560
STUDENT: So just
start over here now?

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:15.120
AMIT GANDHI: Yeah, but I mean,
drop more wire between the two.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:16.950
STUDENT: Oh, so we
can lay them out?

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:17.700
AMIT GANDHI: Yeah.

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:19.513
Because you want to
lay them like that.

00:04:19.513 --> 00:04:21.930
And so you just need enough
wire to go from here, to here,

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:23.093
to here, to here.

00:04:23.093 --> 00:04:23.760
STUDENT: Oh, OK.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:26.190
So I can take this off?

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:29.560
AMIT GANDHI: Well, you
could just pull on--

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:30.750
STUDENT: Move it over here?

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:34.070
AMIT GANDHI: Or you could
just leave this much wire,

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:35.737
then start your
next coil like that.

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:52.465
STUDENT: OK, should I--

00:04:52.465 --> 00:04:55.310
we probably need to wrap a
few of these just a couple

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:56.245
more times.

00:04:56.245 --> 00:04:57.175
STUDENT: Mhm.

00:04:57.175 --> 00:04:58.900
STUDENT: I'd like
to cut this off.

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:06.414
[SIDE CONVERSATIONS]

00:05:09.235 --> 00:05:11.260
STUDENT: OK, ready?

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:12.510
STUDENT: Oh, we need the glue.

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:14.126
Got it.

00:05:14.126 --> 00:05:15.550
STUDENT: Can I steal a glove?

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:19.500
STUDENT: Oh, yeah, but did
they break the wire off there?

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:23.490
STUDENT: This is definitely
not designed for manufacturing.

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:25.350
STUDENT: No.

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:30.460
STUDENT: Oh, we glued it too
tall, guys, or too low down.

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:32.100
STUDENT: What happened?

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:33.600
STUDENT: It must
have slid a little.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:35.130
It's now hitting the thing.

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:35.910
STUDENT: Oh, good.

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:37.380
Nice.

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:38.653
STUDENT: Ah, we're so clever.

00:05:38.653 --> 00:05:39.236
STUDENT: Nice.

00:05:39.236 --> 00:05:41.250
STUDENT: I read the post, paper.

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:42.750
STUDENT: Whoa,
somebody in our group

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:43.660
actually read the [INAUDIBLE].

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:44.790
Can you hand me the glue?

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:47.040
STUDENT: So this should be--

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:48.610
[WHIRRING]

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:52.038
STUDENT: You want AC, right?

00:05:52.038 --> 00:05:53.982
[BEEP]

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:02.051
STUDENT: I'm measuring current.

00:06:02.051 --> 00:06:03.634
STUDENT: Oh, you're
measuring current?

00:06:03.634 --> 00:06:05.603
STUDENT: Yeah.

00:06:05.603 --> 00:06:07.520
STUDENT: It doesn't seem
to be doing anything.

00:06:12.431 --> 00:06:15.980
AMIT GANDHI: You are measuring
connectivity across the wire.

00:06:15.980 --> 00:06:17.780
STUDENT: We measured that, yeah.

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:21.140
There's 17 ohms across
it, which probably

00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:26.000
makes sense for the amount
of tiny wire we have.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:28.160
STUDENT: No, we're
measuring amperes

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:30.850
STUDENT: What?

00:06:30.850 --> 00:06:33.823
[CLATTERING]

00:06:33.823 --> 00:06:34.740
STUDENT: There you go.

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:35.960
That was our mistake.

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:36.690
Never mind.

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:42.618
[INAUDIBLE]

00:06:42.618 --> 00:06:45.810
STUDENT: It's really hot.

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:48.537
The [INAUDIBLE] it's the
core variant of the current.

00:06:48.537 --> 00:06:49.370
[SIDE CONVERSATIONS]

00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:50.940
STUDENT: I have
something backwards.

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:53.138
I have something backwards.

00:06:56.962 --> 00:07:01.760
I'm going to go get
some more light.

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:04.273
STUDENT: Well, it's
pretty far now, actually.

00:07:04.273 --> 00:07:04.940
STUDENT: Really?

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:06.165
So we can put this together?

00:07:06.165 --> 00:07:08.040
STUDENT: So we should
try to straighten this,

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:09.362
you know what I mean?

00:07:09.362 --> 00:07:09.945
STUDENT: Yeah.

00:07:09.945 --> 00:07:11.871
STUDENT: Can we have
the electric tube back?

00:07:14.438 --> 00:07:16.230
STUDENT: This close
to you is [INAUDIBLE]..

00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:17.822
I don't care if
it's close to me.

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:25.420
STUDENT: But it's just tilted.

00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:26.875
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:07:26.875 --> 00:07:29.350
So if you see that one moving.

00:07:29.350 --> 00:07:31.710
So then if we just stick that--

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:36.237
STUDENT: Every time you
pull, you move the coil.

00:07:36.237 --> 00:07:37.570
STUDENT: We're moving the coils?

00:07:37.570 --> 00:07:39.790
STUDENT: Yeah.

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:41.440
You want to re-tape all of them?

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:44.870
STUDENT: --mine and see
if it's good enough.

00:07:44.870 --> 00:07:46.730
STUDENT: I'm going
to go pull out mine.

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:51.916
[INAUDIBLE] over here.

00:07:51.916 --> 00:07:53.170
I know there's one over there.

00:07:53.170 --> 00:07:53.753
STUDENT: Cool.

00:07:56.760 --> 00:07:58.290
STUDENT: Wow, that
is much better.

00:07:58.290 --> 00:07:59.870
STUDENT: Yeah.

00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:02.010
That one, [INAUDIBLE] make
it a little bit closer.

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:07.950
I don't know if this is going
to be a fine enough rating.

00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:10.780
But we're getting 0.4.

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:13.530
0.4, 0.5, 0.6.

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:14.340
STUDENT: Oh, man.

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:16.350
STUDENT: Awesome.

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:20.030
STUDENT: It's doing 0.4
more than anything else.

00:08:23.050 --> 00:08:25.740
It went to 0.8 for a second.

